{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And I said to my master, Perhaps the woman will not go with me.</b> Rashi explained: 'אֵלַי' is written defective, for Eliezer had a daughter, and so forth. And one asks about this: why was 'אוּלַי' not written defective in the account of Eliezer with Abraham? And I say: had it been written defective there, I would have said that it was written defective for this reason — because Eliezer said, 'she will not be willing to come for me,' and 'אֵלַי' means 'for my sake,' for she will not want to go with a servant like me but rather with princes and nobles. But now that it is written defective in the account of Eliezer with Laban and Bethuel, one cannot explain it thus — for how could he mention his own disgrace before them and give them an opening to say, 'indeed she will not want to go with lowly people such as yourself'? And further, why would he mention before them that he had said such a thing to Abraham? Rather, certainly he said that he has a daughter, and he mentioned this in order to intimidate them: that if she does not want to go with him, Abraham will agree to his own daughter — since he will not find from there according to his wish. And what Abraham answered Eliezer concerning this — 'Take heed to yourself' (Genesis 24:6) in any case — implies that even if she does not go after him, in any event he does not agree to give him Eliezer's daughter in marriage. It may be that he said this so that Eliezer would not be negligent in the matter and rely on this — and it is easy to understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:47.495361690Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}